.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt ELF_SECTION_LMA 3
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm elf_section_set_lma ,
.Nm elf_section_lma
.Nd where a section is loaded, when not at its address
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libelfobj
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In elfobj.h
.Ft elf_err_t
.Fn elf_section_set_lma "elf_section_t *section" "uint64_t lma"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn elf_section_lma "const elf_section_t *section"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A section's load address is where its contents are put in memory to begin
with, which may differ from the address it runs at
.Pq Fn elf_section_addr :
a linker script gives it with
.Ql AT(lma) .
The i386 Linux kernel, for one, runs at 0xc1000000 and is loaded at
0x1000000.
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_set_lma
sets
.Fa section Ns 's
load address.
When an executable or shared object is written, each loadable segment's
.Dv p_paddr
is its lowest-addressed section's load address, less the section's
distance from the start of the segment; for a segment whose first section
has none,
.Dv p_paddr
is
.Dv p_vaddr .
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_lma
returns
.Fa section Ns 's
load address: the one set, else its address.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn elf_section_set_lma
returns
.Dv ELF_OK ,
or
.Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
for a
.Dv NULL
section or one of an object opened read-only.
.Fn elf_section_lma
returns the load address, or 0 for a
.Dv NULL
section.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Load a section a fixed distance below where it runs.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <elfobj.h>

void
load_low(elf_section_t *text)
{
    elf_section_set_addr(text, 0xc1000000);
    elf_section_set_lma(text, elf_section_addr(text) - 0xc0000000);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ld 1 ,
.Xr elf_section 3 ,
.Xr elf_section_addr 3
